# Veripsa release gate (auto-discovered). One gate per file -> two PRs adding gates add two FILES, never a
# shared line -> no false merge conflict on registration. register_gate is defined in run_gates.sh.
# Format: register_gate <test> <marker> <name> <ok_blurb> <desc> [tail]
# NOTE: NO backticks and NO dollar-sign in the desc/blurb text below (shell-eval fragility -- see CLAUDE.md).
register_gate "tests/test_allrepos_onboard.py" "ALLREPOS ONBOARD ISOLATION GATE: PASS" "All-repositories install onboards each repo in its OWN locked transaction (one poison repo cannot block the whole org)" "All-repos install per-repo onboarding isolation: GitHub OMITS the repositories array from the installation.created webhook when the install scope is 'All repositories' (repository_selection == 'all') -- the DEFAULT, most-common org install. event_processor.make_db_processor already onboards an EXPLICIT-list install through _process_install_event_per_repo_locked (each repo in its OWN advisory lock + OWN connection + OWN transaction, so one repo's DB error can never roll back the others). But the ALL-REPOS install named no repos, so the fan-out returned nothing and the event FELL THROUGH to the SINGLE shared body transaction (repo None -> NO lock): handle_event -> ingest._onboard_repos then ENUMERATES the install's repos (gh.installation_repos) and cold-starts up to the onboard cap (~50) of them ALL inside that ONE transaction. A single DB error on ONE repo mid-onboarding ABORTED the shared transaction -> every LATER repo's writes failed with current-transaction-is-aborted -> the final commit rolled the WHOLE org's onboarding back. One poison repo SILENTLY blocked the entire org from ever onboarding. THE FIX (event_processor.make_db_processor): when an install-level ONBOARDING action (created / unsuspend / new_permissions_accepted) named no repos, ENUMERATE the install's repos via gh.installation_repos (the SAME source boot_reconcile / _onboard_repos use) and route them through the SAME per-repo LOCKED fan-out the explicit-list path uses -- each repo onboards in its OWN lock, connection, and explicit body transaction, so one repo's failure is contained and its own partial writes roll back while the rest still onboard. Bounded by the eager onboard cap (a huge org cannot make one install storm the API; the rest defer to first push) and FAIL-OPEN (enumerate nothing -> the empty-onboard body, never crash). Purge / delta actions are excluded (they name repos authoritatively; the account-wide uninstall is deliberately not fanned out), so this changes ONLY the all-repos onboarding path; the explicit-list path is byte-for-byte unchanged. WHAT THIS GATE PROVES (driven through the REAL make_db_processor over the REAL gate, authed as the REAL least-privilege role veripsa_app, against a scratch Postgres -- the exact prod transaction model): (1) an All-repos install whose enumerated set is [good-A, POISON, good-C] where POISON raises a real DB error mid-onboarding STILL onboards the OTHER repos (their graph is committed); (2) the poison repo itself did NOT onboard; (3) it really took the all-repos branch; (4) a separate post-ingest stable-identity failure escapes the bounded sweep for durable retry; (5) fresh successful siblings from that failure scenario commit independently; (6) the failed repo's already-written graph is rolled back atomically. Content-free throughout (repo full_names + committed-graph readback only, never bodies); advisory + fail-open unchanged." 24
